    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p52-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "What is the concentration of the antibiotic after 1 hour? Give your answer to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1.34\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}C(1)&=9\\left(e^{-0.2\\times1}-e^{-0.4\\times1}\\right)\\\\C(1)&\\approx1.34\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Let $x=e^{-0.2t}$ and show that when $C=2$, the resultant quadratic is $9x^2-9x+2=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$e^{-0.4t}=x^2$, so $2=9(x-x^2)$, giving $9x^2-9x+2=0$.",
          "worked": "Let $x=e^{-0.2t}$. Then $e^{-0.4t}=(e^{-0.2t})^2=x^2$, so $C=9(x-x^2)$.\n\nWhen $C=2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2&=9(x-x^2)\\\\2&=9x-9x^2\\\\9x^2-9x+2&=0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Solve for $x$, and hence show that it will take just over 2 hours to reach $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$. Give times to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\frac13$ or $\\frac23$. The two times are $5\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac32\\right)\\approx2.03$ hours and $5\\ln3\\approx5.49$ hours. The concentration first reaches $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ just over 2 hours after introduction.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}9x^2-9x+2&=0\\\\(3x-1)(3x-2)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x=\\frac13$ or $x=\\frac23$.\n\nFor $x=\\frac23$:\n$$\\begin{align*}e^{-0.2t}&=\\frac23\\\\-0.2t&=\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac23\\right)\\\\t&=-\\frac{\\ln(2/3)}{0.2}\\\\t&\\approx2.03\\text{ hours}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $x=\\frac13$:\n$$\\begin{align*}e^{-0.2t}&=\\frac13\\\\-0.2t&=\\ln\\!\\left(\\frac13\\right)\\\\t&=-\\frac{\\ln(1/3)}{0.2}\\\\t&\\approx5.49\\text{ hours}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe first time is $2.03$ hours, which is just over 2 hours.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p52-q4-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "You should have acquired two solutions. What could be a biological reason as to why there may be two times when the concentration is $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The concentration rises as the antibiotic is absorbed, then falls as it is metabolised. It reaches the same concentration once while rising and once while falling.",
          "worked": "Initially, absorption of the antibiotic causes its concentration in the bloodstream to rise. After reaching a peak, the concentration falls as the antibiotic is metabolised. It therefore reaches $2\\,\\mu\\mathrm{g}\\,\\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ once while rising and again while falling.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      }
    ]
